    36	itself — which becomes v2 — or a namespace in this table. Nothing dangles.
    37	
    38	| v2 namespace | ported from | lines | file |
    39	|---|---|---|---|
    40	| `dao.datom.v2` | `dao.datom` | 91 | `src/cljc/dao/datom/v2.cljc` |
    41	| `dao.runtime` | `dao.runtime` | 258 | `src/cljc/dao/runtime.cljc` |
    42	| `dao.stream.apply` | `dao.stream.apply` | 231 | `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc` |
    43	| `yin.vm` | `yin.vm` | 554 | `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc` |
    44	| `yin.vm.telemetry` | `yin/vm/telemetry` | 282 | `src/cljc/yin/vm/telemetry.cljc` |
    45	| `yin.vm.runtime-adapter` | `yin/vm/runtime_adapter` | 47 | `src/cljc/yin/vm/runtime_adapter.cljc` |
    46	| `yin.vm.stream-driver` | `yin/vm/stream_driver` | 59 | `src/cljc/yin/vm/stream_driver.cljc` |
    47	| `yin.vm.engine` | `yin/vm/engine` | 671 | `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` |
    48	| `yin.vm.ffi` | `yin/vm/ffi` | 154 | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc` |
    49	| `yin.vm.ast-walker` | `yin/vm/ast_walker` | 795 | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc` |
    50	
    51	**~3,142 lines**, plus fresh tests.
    52	
    53	Two notes on the table. **`yin.module` (114 lines) is reused, not forked** — it
    54	requires only `clojure.string`, has no v1 coupling of any kind, and a copy would
    55	be identical code carrying drift risk for no benefit. And **`dao.datom.v2`
    56	starts as a verbatim copy**: `dao.datom` has zero `dao.stream` references, so
    57	there is nothing in it to port. It is forked to keep the v2 tree free of v1
    58	requires, which is a boundary decision rather than a code change.
    59	
    60	## What actually changes
    61	
    62	The port is not a rename. Six v1 idioms run through this code, and each is
    63	something the contract retired. Finding them all is most of the work.
    64	
    65	**Cursors are minted, never fabricated.** `stream_driver.cljc:25` reads
    66	`(or (:in-cursor vm) {:position 0})`, and `dao.stream.apply` defaults the same
    67	way at lines 170 and 184. In v2 there is no `seek` and no position: a cursor
    68	comes from `cursor` with an anchor, or from a `next` result, or from a gap's
    69	recovery cursor. The VM's `:in-cursor` becomes an opaque value it stores and
    70	hands back.
    71	
    72	**Outcomes are maps, not sentinels.** v1 `next` returns bare `:blocked`, `:end`,
    73	`:daostream/gap`, or `{:ok v :cursor c}` — `stream_driver.cljc:28-40` dispatches
    74	on `map?` and keyword equality, and `apply.cljc:165-187` documents passing those
    75	sentinels through unchanged. In v2 every result is a map keyed by
    76	`:dao.stream/outcome`, carrying `:dao.stream/value` and `:dao.stream/cursor`.
    77	Every such dispatch is rewritten, and the outcome set is closed, so each site
    78	handles `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` and `transport-error` too.
    79	
    80	**Creation is not attachment.** `vm.cljc:139` calls
    81	`ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer, :mode :create, :capacity nil}`. v2
    82	splits that into `create!` and `attach!`, so `:mode` disappears, and retention
    83	is declared rather than omitted: **the VM's in-stream is created with a declared
    84	capacity**, recorded in this plan as 4096 elements.
    85	
    86	**`append!` outcomes are read.** `telemetry.cljc:281` and the FFI's
    87	`put-request!` / `put-response!` (`apply.cljc:146,157`) discard what `append!`
    88	returns. In v2 a `full` means nothing was appended, and telemetry that silently
    89	drops a datom is a defect rather than backpressure.
    90	
    91	**Stream-ness is a surface question.** `telemetry.cljc:124,166` classify a value
    92	with `satisfies? ds/IDaoStreamReader` to decide whether something in the VM store
    93	is a stream. The v2 equivalent asks the same question of the v2 reader protocol.
    94	This is legitimate — a declared surface is exactly what a handle publishes — and
    95	is not the forbidden reaching for a transport-owned operation.
    96	
    97	**Ingress polling stays guarded.** `engine.cljc:310` polls only when an
    98	in-stream is present, `(if (and in-stream (ready-for-ingress? v)) …)`. That
    99	guard is why the REPL can run a v2 VM with no in-stream at all, and it is
   100	preserved.
   101	
   102	## `dao.stream.apply`
   103	
   104	The FFI bridge is not optional: `ast_walker` runs through `ffi/maybe-run`, and
   105	`ffi` needs a request/response protocol over a stream pair. `dao.stream.apply`
   106	is that protocol — an endpoint is `{request-stream, response-stream}`, requests
   107	and responses are tagged maps with an id, an op and args, and `serve-once!`
   108	reads one request and writes one response.
   109	
   110	The v2 version keeps that shape and fixes what the contract retired: minted
   111	cursors instead of `{:position 0}` defaults, outcome maps instead of passed-
   112	through sentinels, and read `append!` results. It also gains what a step-driven
   113	world needs — `serve-once!` becomes a step that returns its next cursor and
   114	state rather than looping.
   115	
   116	**Its envelope design is shared with the REPL's RPC layer.** Both are
   117	request/response over a pair of streams with correlation ids, and
   118	`yin.repl.implementation-plan.md` fixes an equivalent envelope in its own R1.
   119	They should agree on id representation and error encoding, under their own
   120	keys. Divergence here is two designs for one problem.
   121	
   122	## Prerequisites
   123	
   124	From `dao.stream.implementation-plan.md`, and **only** these:
   125	
   126	- **Phase 1, completed** — the protocols, the result convention, and the
   127	  conformance harness it defines.
   128	- **Phase 2, completed** — the ring buffer, with `create!`, both anchors,
   129	  evict-oldest with declared capacity, and `gap` with a recovery cursor.
   130	
   131	Not needed: Phase 3, Phase 4 in any part, Phase 5, and both decision gates.
   132	Nothing here attaches to anything, crosses a wire, or names a descriptor. **This
   133	plan is buildable earlier than any part of the REPL slice**, and is the shortest
   134	path to a real consumer of the v2 contract.
   135	
   136	## Phase V1 — The leaves
   137	
   138	The three namespaces with no v2-specific behaviour of their own.
   139	
   140	- `dao.datom.v2` — verbatim copy, no changes.
   141	- `yin.vm` — the datom model, schema and primitives. The one substantive
   142	  change is the in-stream `create!` with declared capacity.
   143	- `dao.runtime` — the ready-queue and wait-set state, with its single
   144	  `dao.stream` reference moved to v2.
   145	
   146	Deliverable: three namespaces compiling on clj, cljs and cljd, with tests for
   147	`yin.vm`'s datom conversion and primitives.
   148	
   149	## Phase V2 — The stream seam
   150	
   151	- `yin.vm.stream-driver` — `ready-for-ingress?` unchanged;
   152	  `ingest-next-program` rewritten around minted cursors and outcome maps, total
   153	  over `ok`, `blocked`, `end`, `gap`, `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` and
   154	  `transport-error`. A `gap` on the in-stream is a program batch that was
   155	  evicted before the VM ingested it: it resumes at the recovery cursor and
   156	  records the loss, rather than treating it as `end`.
   157	- `yin.vm.telemetry` — the emit path with read `append!` outcomes, and
   158	  stream classification against the v2 reader protocol. Its stream is created
   159	  with a declared capacity, recorded here as 4096.
   160	- `yin.vm.runtime-adapter` — thin, follows `dao.runtime`.
   161	
   162	Deliverable: the driver ingesting a program batch from a v2 ring buffer, and
   163	telemetry emitting to one, on all three hosts. This is where the port's real
   164	work is, and it is testable with no evaluator at all.
   165	
   166	## Phase V3 — `dao.stream.apply` and the FFI
   167	
   168	- `dao.stream.apply` per the section above, with its envelope agreed against
   169	  the REPL plan's.
   170	- `yin.vm.ffi` — the bridge state and `maybe-run`, over the v2 endpoint.
   171	
   172	Deliverable: a host call round-tripping through a v2 endpoint pair, with no
   173	evaluator: a request written, read as a step, dispatched, and its response read
   174	back.
   175	
   176	## Phase V4 — The engine and the evaluator
   177	
   178	- `yin.vm.engine` — `run-loop`, `run-on-stream` with its ingress guard
   179	  preserved, and the telemetry hooks.
   180	- `yin.vm.ast-walker` — `create-vm` and the evaluation protocol.
   181	
   182	Deliverable: `(-> (ast-walker/create-vm …) (vm/eval ast) (vm/run))` producing the
   183	same results the v1 ast-walker produces for the same programs, on all three
   184	hosts.
   185	
   186	## Phase V5 — Evidence
   187	
   188	Fresh tests for what v2 implements; the v1 suite is untouched and keeps testing
   189	v1.
   190	
   191	- Unit tests per namespace, mirroring the v1 suite's coverage **for the
   192	  subsystems in scope only** — no tests for `macro`, `space`, `wasm`, or the
   193	  three unbuilt evaluators.
   194	- The stream-seam tests are the ones that matter most, because that is what the
   195	  port changed: ingress across a gap, telemetry under a full destination, and
   196	  the FFI round trip.
   197	- An evaluation suite over the ast-walker, drawn from the programs
   198	  `test/yin/vm/ast_walker_test.cljc` covers, written fresh against the v2 API.
   199	
   200	**No parity test against v1.** It would require both VMs loadable in one
   201	process, and v1's ast-walker reached through v1 `dao.stream`; the coupling that
   202	would create is exactly what this plan exists to avoid. Divergence is caught by
   203	the evaluation suite covering the same programs, not by running both.
   204	
   205	## Host matrix
   206	
   207	clj, cljs (Node) and cljd, all three, every phase — the same standard the REPL
   208	slice holds, and cheap here because nothing in this plan touches a socket.
   209	
   210	`#?(:clj …)` does **not** exclude code from the cljd build; `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`
   211	with `:cljd` **first** is required, and `:cljd` in tail position silently fails.
   212	The v1 sources being ported contain bare `#?(:clj …)` forms — `vm.cljc:6-9` among
   213	them — which must be rewritten as they are copied. Full cljd namespace
   214	compilation gates each phase.
   215	
   216	## Boundary of this plan
   217	
   218	**Untouched:** `yin.vm`, everything under `src/cljc/yin/vm/`, `dao.datom`,
   219	`dao.runtime`, `dao.stream.apply`, `yin.module`, and their tests. The fifteen
   220	existing `yin.vm` consumers — the demos, benches, telemetry servers,
   221	`yang.clojure`, and the v1 REPL — keep using v1 and are not migrated here.
   222	
   223	**Not in this plan:** `semantic`, `register` and `stack`; `macro`, `space` and
   224	`wasm`; `dao.space` in any form; migration of any existing consumer; and the
   225	`dao.stream.cljc` → `yin.vm` cycle that exists in v1, which v2 does not
   226	reproduce and does not fix for v1.
   227	
   228	## End condition
   229	
   230	Complete when, on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd, a `yin.vm.ast-walker` evaluates
   231	programs and runs its ingress, telemetry and FFI paths over v2 streams, with no
   232	namespace under `yin.vm` or `dao.*.v2` requiring v1 `dao.stream`,
   233	`dao.datom`, `dao.runtime`, `dao.stream.apply`, or any `yin.vm` namespace.
   234	
   235	Coexistence is the expected end state. `yin.repl` is the first consumer;
   236	`semantic`, `register` and `stack` follow, and bring the `dao.space` question
   237	with them.

