   131	## The v2 RPC layer
   132	
   133	`dao.stream.rpc.*` cannot be ported. The diagnosis was checked against the code
   134	by four reviewers and holds: v1 reads responses from the same duplex handle it
   135	wrote to (`rpc/client.cljc:155` through `WebSocketStream`'s hidden
   136	`:remote-stream` inbox, `ws.cljc:68` — the very thing `dao.stream.ws.md:160`
   137	forbids); its cursors are `(:position …)` arithmetic seeded from
   138	`(atom {:position 0})` (`rpc/client.cljc:29-42, 129`); and it returns a Promise
   139	on cljs and a Future on cljd (`rpc/client.cljc:55-57, 108-111`).
   140	
   141	**A client is constructed from two handles, not one** — a writer for requests,
   142	and a reader plus cursor for the response medium. That asymmetry is the v2
   143	shape: under a transport with no reader surface the two directions are two
   144	streams, and pretending otherwise is what produced the hidden inbox.
   145	
   146	**Client state, explicitly:**
   147	
   148	```clojure
   149	{:writer      <handle>          ; request path
   150	 :reader      <handle>          ; response medium
   151	 :cursor      <cursor>          ; the successor next returned; never arithmetic
   152	 :me          <attachment-id>   ; nil on a private medium
   153	 :decode      <fn>              ; envelope -> response, or nil for bare values
   154	 :outstanding {id {:op … :args …}}
   155	 :unsent      nil-or-{:id … :op … :args …}
   156	 :completed   [ … ]}
   157	```
   158	
   159	- **`request!`** appends and returns the next state. On `:dao.stream/full`
   160	  nothing was appended: the request and **its already-allocated id** are
   161	  retained in `:unsent`, and the next `request!` retries that one rather than
   162	  minting a new id. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are terminal
   163	  for that request.
   164	- **`poll!`** drains the response medium up to a budget through its own cursor,
   165	  classifies each element, matches responses to `:outstanding`, and returns the
   166	  next state plus completions. `:dao.stream/blocked` returns to the caller; that
   167	  is the yield, and the caller must then return to its host loop.
   168	- **One driver owns client state and threads it serially.** Two callers holding
   169	  the same immutable state mint the same next id and produce divergent states.
   170	  Several requests may be outstanding at once — ids are matched at drain time,
   171	  which removes the whole bug class v1's `advance-cursor!` monotonic-max surgery
   172	  exists to patch — but only under one poller.
   173	- **Timeouts are the driver's, not the layer's.** A step-driven layer has no
   174	  clock; deadlines are the caller's, and `now` is passed in if the layer needs
   175	  it at all.
   176	- **Loss is conservative.** On a `:dao.stream/gap` the recovery cursor says
   177	  nothing about which responses were skipped, so **every outstanding request is
   178	  reported lost**, not a computed subset. The same applies on any terminal
   179	  lifecycle event for this attachment — `:ws/closed`, `:ws/not-found`,
   180	  `:ws/transport-error` — which must be reported as loss rather than left to
   181	  time out.
   182	- **Reattachment is a rebind.** `rebind` swaps a dead writer for a fresh
   183	  `attach!` result and takes the new `:me`, keeping the cursor. Without it the
   184	  client filters on a stale attachment id and drops everything the new boundary
   185	  deposits.
   186	
   187	**The response medium carries envelopes, not responses.** On a real socket every
   188	element is `{:ws/attachment … :ws/event … :ws/value …}`, mixed across
   189	attachments and interleaved with `:ws/opened`, `:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`,
   190	`:ws/not-found` and `:ws/transport-error`. `poll!` must filter to `:me`, advance
   191	past other attachments' events without treating them as responses, unwrap only
   192	`:ws/payload`, and convert lifecycle events into client transitions.
   193	`dao.stream.rpc.ws` supplies that `:decode` and consumes the **whole**
   194	`attach!` result including `:dao.stream/attachment`. R1 tested over bare ring
   195	buffers passes and then breaks at R3 unless this seam is built in R1.
   196	
   197	**Server state, explicitly:**
   198	
   199	```clojure
   200	{:request-cursor      <cursor>
   201	 :pending-response    nil-or-<encoded>
   202	 :pending-request-id  nil-or-<id>}
   203	```
   204	
   205	`serve-step` retries `:pending-response` **before** reading another request. A
   206	response `append!` that returns `full` must not advance the request cursor — and
   207	must not re-run the handler either, because Yin evaluation is stateful and
   208	re-execution is wrong. v1 sidesteps this by ignoring the write result
   209	(`rpc/server.cljc:65`); that cannot be copied into an API claiming totality.
   210	Each request advances the cursor once and executes its handler once.
   211	
   212	**The envelope is fixed in R1**: request and response value shapes, correlation
   213	id representation, and handler-error encoding, under `:dao.stream.rpc/…`
   214	keys. v1's `dao.stream.apply` maps (`apply.cljc:42-63`) and its `{:error msg}`
   215	wrapping (`rpc/server.cljc:24-38`) are the obvious model, copied under v2 keys
   216	rather than required from v1.
   217	
   218	**Not in the v2 layer:** `retry` and `dedup`, which exist for the lossy UDP
   219	transport that v2 does not have. They stay on v1 with `dao.stream.rpc.udp`.
   220	
   221	## The REPL driver
   222	
   223	The first draft asserted that the REPL "already owns a polling loop." It does
   224	not, and never did: `rpc-client/wait-for-response` owned it
   225	(`rpc/client.cljc:77-111`) and the REPL consumed its Promise or Future
   226	(`repl.cljc:526-546`, with the cljs main's re-prompt promise-chained at 903 and
   227	the cljd main's at 1044). The three main loops are **line-event loops**, not
   228	response-poll loops.
   229	
   230	So the driver is a deliverable, not a caller convenience. `yin.repl.driver`
   231	owns one step:
   232	
   233	```clojure
   234	;; Called once per externally driven tick; never loops on :blocked.
   235	(defn repl-step [state now] …)
   236	```
   237	
   238	which drains the output stream, polls the RPC client, advances any server
   239	sessions, publishes completions to the printer, and returns the next state. It
   240	never loops on `blocked`.
   241	
   242	**Per host, because cadence is the runtime's** (`dao.stream.md:178-180`):
   243	
   244	- **clj** — `run-cli!` parks in `read-line` (`repl.cljc:856`). One owned poller
   245	  thread runs `repl-step` on an interval so `:ws/opened` and remote results
   246	  print when they arrive rather than after the user's next keystroke. A caller
   247	  wanting a blocking wait for one response may write a sleep loop; that is
   248	  ordinary control flow on a host that can park.
   249	- **cljs (Node)** — the readline line handler must **return** after `request!`.
   250	  A single `setInterval` owns `repl-step`, printing, and the prompt discipline,
   251	  replacing the promise chain at `repl.cljc:903`.
   252	- **cljd** — identically, with `Timer.periodic`, replacing the chain at
   253	  `repl.cljc:1044`. A synchronous `(loop [] (poll!) (recur))` deadlocks the Dart
   254	  event loop: ws IO never progresses, so `blocked` never clears.
   255	
   256	**One poller, one client state.** Two tickers on one client deliver completions
   257	to the wrong consumer.
   258	
   259	**`eval-input`'s contract changes and must be stated**: local evaluation returns
   260	a value; remote evaluation returns immediately with a request id, and the result
   261	is printed by the driver when it completes. Input typed while a request is
   262	outstanding is queued, not evaluated — v1 has a clobber race there
   263	(`repl.cljc:1044-1047`) that must not be copied.
   264	
   265	The claim that host divergence disappears is **withdrawn**. It moves: out of the
   266	RPC layer, which now has one shape everywhere, and into one small named driver
   267	per host. That is the reduction this plan actually delivers.
   268	
   269	## Decisions
   270	
   271	**D2 — a reply travels on the connection's server-side handle. Settled.**
   272	The accepted-connection handle is writer+closable with its writer surface on
   273	that connection's ordered outbound path (`dao.stream.ws.md:44-48`), so replies
   274	are private by construction. The alternative — a per-conversation served stream
   275	driven by `forward-step` — buys an outbound replay history that is worthless
   276	until resumption is specified, and resumption is deferred. `forward-step` (4b)
   277	is therefore genuinely unnecessary here.
   278	
   279	**D3 — `/repl` names a service-lifetime stream. Settled now, not deferred.**
   280	The first draft chose D2a and left D3 open, which does not work: a descriptor
   281	names a server-hosted stream that exists independently of connections and that
   282	the same descriptor reaches every time (`dao.stream.ws.md:9-16`), so with
   283	nothing served the handshake must authoritatively disclaim `/repl` and *every*
   284	`connect` deposits `:ws/not-found`.
   285	
   286	So the endpoint `create!`s **one composition-owned ring buffer at start**,
   287	registers it in the resolution table under `/repl`, and both the client handle
   288	and each accepted-connection handle answer `descriptor` with it
   289	(`dao.stream.ws.md:38-42`). It carries no ordinary outbound values in this
   290	slice — nothing is forwarded into it, which is composition policy and not a
   291	spec violation. Closing it is the endpoint's `stop!`, which closes each
   292	attachment with the ended-stream close code, and that is what makes R5's fourth
   293	fact implementable at all.
   294	
   295	Only the descriptor **key names** wait for the gate. The shape is settled here.
   296	A pathless `daostream:ws://host:port` normalizes to the `/repl` path.
   297	
   298	**D4 — one shared shell, and evaluation is serialized. Settled.**
   299	v1 builds every handler over one `state-atom` (`repl.cljc:782`) and the `:op/eval`
   300	handler reads and resets it (`repl.cljc:734`). Independent per-connection loops
   301	would race on VM state; correlating replies by attachment does not prevent that.
   302	The v2 server therefore runs **one driver** that fairly advances every session
   303	and threads a single explicit REPL state serially. Connecting clients share one
   304	remote shell, which is what v1 does today and what the existing tests assume —
   305	they send sequentially (`repl_test.cljc:581`), so they never settled it.
   306	
   307	**Chain-forwarding is out.** v1's `:op/eval` handler forwards to the server's
   308	own remote endpoint when one is connected, and that is tested
   309	(`repl_test.cljc:320`). A step-driven handler cannot produce that answer
   310	synchronously without a multi-stage handler state. The v2 server evaluates
   311	locally or reports that it does not proxy.
   312	
   313	## Prerequisites
   314	
   315	From [`yin.vm.implementation-plan.md`](./yin.vm.implementation-plan.md):
   316	
   317	- **The whole of it** — `yin.vm.ast-walker` and its closure, through Phase
   318	  V4. It needs only the sibling stream plan's Phases 1 and 2, so it can be built
   319	  in parallel with this plan's R1 and must land before R2.
   320	
   321	From `dao.stream.implementation-plan.md`:
   322	
   323	- **Phase 1 and Phase 2, completed** — the protocols, the result convention, and
   324	  the ring buffer, including the conformance harness that Phase 1 defines and
   325	  Phase 2 first runs. If R1 depends on a completed Phase 2 then it depends on
   326	  the harness too; the earlier draft's exclusion of it was wrong.
   327	- **Phase 4a, the ws transport, both ends**, on clj and cljs.
   328	- **The cljd v2 ws transport**, which the sibling plan defers and **this plan
   329	  owns**, over `dart:io`. It owes the same manifest and conformance evidence as
   330	  the other two hosts.
   331	- **Both decision gates**: the descriptor key set, and the wire contract.
   332	
   333	**Not prerequisites:** Phase 3 (the REPL attaches to a ws endpoint by URL, never
   334	to a ring buffer by descriptor), 4b (dead under D2a), 4c (R4 builds the REPL's
   335	own serving composition, which re-derives 4c's list minus the forwarder), and
   336	Phase 5 (this plan is that proof).
   337	
   338	**Blocked, not merely sequenced.** Three answers do not exist in any document
   339	today, and R3 and R4 cannot be built without them. Each needs an amendment to
   340	`dao.stream.ws.md`, which this plan cannot make while subordinate to it:
   341	
   342	1. **Server-side attachment identity** — the contract defines
   343	   `:dao.stream/attachment` only in an `attach!` success map, and a server
   344	   handle is minted from a handed-over socket with no `attach!` call.
   345	2. **Accept notification** — how a composition learns a connection was accepted,
   346	   and receives its writer handle, without the `:on-connect` callback the spec
   347	   forbids. This is more basic than identity and nothing addresses it.
   348	3. **The wire contract's contents** — handshake presentation, disclaimer form,
   349	   ended-stream close code, value codec, decode-failure behaviour.
   350	
   351	**R1 and R2 are executable today** once the envelope is fixed; R3 through R5 are
   352	gated on the three above. That split is real and this plan states it rather than
   353	sequencing past it.
   354	
   355	## Phase R1 — `dao.stream.rpc`
   356	
   357	Over two ring buffers, no socket.
   358	
   359	- Fix the request/response envelope, correlation ids, and error encoding —
   360	  **agreed with `dao.stream.apply`'s**, since both are request/response over a
   361	  stream pair and two designs for one problem is a defect. Each keeps its own
   362	  key namespace.
   363	- `client.cljc`: `init-client`, `request!`, `poll!`, `rebind`, `close!`, with the
   364	  state above. No `{:position …}` anywhere, no promise on any host.
   365	- `server.cljc`: `serve-step` with pending-response retry.
   366	- `ws.cljc`: the `:decode` that filters by `:me`, unwraps `:ws/payload`, and
   367	  converts lifecycle events — written here even though its transport arrives in
   368	  R3, and tested with hand-built envelopes over a ring buffer.
   369	- Total over both outcome algebras, including the conservative gap rule.
   370	
   371	Deliverable: request/response over two ring buffers on clj, cljs and cljd, plus
   372	an envelope-decode test, with one shape on all three.
   373	
   374	## Phase R2 — `yin.repl` and its driver, local only
   375	
   376	No socket, no wire, no RPC.
   377	
   378	- **The function-level inventory** of `yin/repl.cljc` first; then
   379	  `yin.repl.core` and `yin.repl`.
   380	- `make-vm` constructs a `yin.vm.ast-walker`, hands it a v2 in-stream, and
   381	  installs v2 telemetry when asked. `eval-datoms` appends to that in-stream and
   382	  `(telemetry)` works locally, per *The VM*. `vm-constructors` has one entry.
   383	- The output stream is a v2 ring buffer with a **declared capacity of 4096
   384	  elements**; its drain loop mints a cursor with `cursor` and advances by the
   385	  successor `next` returns. A `gap` there prints an explicit loss notice and
   386	  resumes at the recovery cursor. `end`, `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` and
   387	  `transport-error` each get a stated response. (The sibling Phase 2 declares
   388	  only evict-oldest with declared capacity; nothing here needs an unbounded
   389	  mode, so none is requested.)
   390	- **`repl-state` without `closed?`.** The state carries a per-stream last-outcome
   391	  ledger with an explicit `:untried` value, since an idle stream has no last
   392	  operation and there is nothing to ask.
   393	- `yin.repl.driver` with `repl-step`, and the per-host tickers.
   394	- The four `deps.edn` aliases and the `shadow-cljs.edn` build **land here**, not
   395	  in R5, because R2's deliverable is stated in terms of them.
   396	
   397	Deliverable: a working local REPL on all three hosts — `clj -M:clj-yin-repl`,
   398	the `:yin-repl` node build, `clj -M:cljd-yin-repl` — requiring no v1
   399	namespace.
   400	
   401	## Phase R3 — The client side
   402	
   403	- `connect` normalizes the URL to a descriptor whose keys come from the settled
   404	  gate, calls `attach!`, and gets a handle at once. No promise on any host.
   405	- The composition wires the deposit medium — **one medium per boundary**,
   406	  capacity 8192, matching 4a and demultiplexed by `:ws/attachment`, which the
   407	  client already does. Per-attachment media would need the boundary-level
   408	  announcement stream `dao.stream.ws.md:140-143` requires, which 4a does not
   409	  build.
   410	- The RPC client is built from the ws handle, that medium, a minted cursor
   411	  anchored at `:dao.stream/newest`, and the whole `attach!` result for `:me`.
   412	- **`Connected to …` moves** to when the driver observes `:ws/opened`.
   413	  `:ws/not-found` reports an authoritative disclaimer and is not retried;
   414	  `:ws/transport-error` reports a reachability failure that may succeed on retry.
   415	- `disconnect` is `close!`. Reattaching is `attach!` plus `rebind`, keeping the
   416	  deposit cursor and taking the new attachment id.
   417	
   418	## Phase R4 — The server side
   419	
   420	- The endpoint creates its `/repl` service-lifetime stream at start (D3) and
   421	  registers it in a host-owned resolution table.
   422	- One request medium per boundary, capacity 8192, demultiplexed by
   423	  `:ws/attachment`.
   424	- One server driver advances every session against a single serially-threaded
   425	  REPL state (D4), running `serve-step` per session.
   426	- Serving lifecycle: start, stop, and who owns them, replacing the `:conns` atom
   427	  and the `:on-connect`/`:on-disconnect` callbacks. `stop!` closes the service
   428	  stream, which closes each attachment with the ended-stream code.
   429	- **On cljd**, `HttpServer.bind` is asynchronous, so `serve!` returns before the
   430	  port is bound. Where bind success, bind failure, upgrade failure, listener
   431	  error, and asynchronous close completion appear **as data** must be defined
   432	  here. Node's `ws` server fails the same way; http-kit throws synchronously.
   433	  v1's cljd `listen!` never sets `:socket-close-fn` (`ws.cljc:396-408`), so
   434	  server-side `close!` never closes the socket — do not copy that.
   435	
   436	## Phase R5 — End to end
   437	
   438	Per host, then across hosts.
   439	
   440	`clj -M:clj-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless`; from a second process
   441	`(connect "daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl")`; evaluate; disconnect;
   442	reconnect. Verify:
   443	
   444	1. Evaluation round-trips, and two clients each get their own answers.
   445	2. Killing the connection is observable on both sides: the client handle answers
   446	   `closed`, the server's boundary deposits the departure, and every outstanding
   447	   request is reported lost rather than timing out.
   448	3. Reattaching with the same descriptor reaches the same served stream, and the
   449	   client's cursor on its own deposit medium resumes across the socket's death.
   450	4. The endpoint's `stop!` closes the service stream, and the client deposits
   451	   `:ws/ended`, not `:ws/closed`.
   452	
   453	Then the cross-host pair: a cljd client against a JVM server, and a Node client
   454	against a cljd server. The descriptor crossed a codec and the wire is the same
   455	wire; if that fails, the contract was implemented three times rather than once.
   456	
   457	`src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md` is written here. The existing
   458	`yin.repl.md` is left alone. The new document states what differs: `connect`
   459	returns immediately and reports its outcome when known, `(vm :type)` offers
   460	`:ast-walker` only, and telemetry has no remote `ws://` sink.
   461	
   462	## Host matrix
   463	
   464	All three hosts, first class, every phase.
   465	
   466	- **Declare the `append!` outcome subset per host, with reasons**, which is what
   467	  the contract asks (`dao.stream.md:417-424`). Transient `full` may not be
   468	  detectable anywhere: `java.net.http sendText` buffers, Node's `ws.send`
   469	  buffers, and Dart's `WebSocket.add` buffers, none exposing backpressure. If
   470	  `full` is excluded by nature on a host, say so with the reason rather than
   471	  inventing a signal.
   472	- On cljd, `add` after close throws `StateError` synchronously and must be
   473	  classified to `:dao.stream/closed`. The codec exists at
   474	  `src/cljd/dao/stream/transit.cljd`.
   475	- Full cljd namespace compilation gates each phase, per *Namespaces and files*.
   476	
   477	## Boundary of this plan
   478	
   479	**Untouched** — no edits, no deletions, no deprecation markers: `dao.stream.cljc`
   480	and everything under `src/cljc/dao/stream/`; `yin/repl.cljc`,
   481	`src/clj/yin/repl/runner.clj`, `src/cljd/yin/repl/flutter.cljd`; their tests;
   482	the existing `deps.edn` aliases and `shadow-cljs.edn` builds. `dao.jing.remote`
   483	keeps working on v1 and gets its own plan; it is the harder migration, since its
   484	`call!` is synchronous-on-JVM and `jing/materialize!` and `jing/get` dispatch
   485	through it as ordinary value-returning calls.
   486	
   487	**Deliberately out of this slice:** remote telemetry over `ws://`, a second ws
   488	client that proves nothing `connect` does not; the `:semantic`, `:register` and
   489	`:stack` evaluators, which follow in the VM plan; chain-forwarding through a
   490	server's own remote (D4); the Flutter widget; `retry`, `dedup` and a v2 UDP
   491	transport; ws-level resumption; flow control; and authentication — `--host
   492	127.0.0.1` remains the only boundary, as in v1.
   493	
   494	## End condition
   495	
   496	Complete when, on each of clj, cljs (Node) and cljd, a v2 REPL server accepts a
   497	connection from a second process, evaluates forms sent to it, and survives a
   498	disconnect and reattach — with `yin.repl` requiring no v1 namespace, which
   499	running on `yin.vm` makes true rather than aspirational. R5's four facts plus
   500	the cross-host pair are the test.
   501	
   502	Coexistence is the expected end state. Both REPLs ship, both alias sets work,
   503	and `dao.stream.rpc.*` keeps serving `dao.jing.remote`. Deleting v1 is the
   504	stream plan's end condition, when its last consumer has migrated — of which this
   505	is the first.

