   131	## The v2 RPC layer
   132	
   133	`dao.stream.rpc.*` cannot be ported. The diagnosis was checked against the code
   134	by four reviewers and holds: v1 reads responses from the same duplex handle it
   135	wrote to (`rpc/client.cljc:155` through `WebSocketStream`'s hidden
   136	`:remote-stream` inbox, `ws.cljc:68` — the very thing `dao.stream.ws.md:160`
   137	forbids); its cursors are `(:position …)` arithmetic seeded from
   138	`(atom {:position 0})` (`rpc/client.cljc:29-42, 129`); and it returns a Promise
   139	on cljs and a Future on cljd (`rpc/client.cljc:55-57, 108-111`).
   140	
   141	**A client is constructed from two handles, not one** — a writer for requests,
   142	and a reader plus cursor for the response medium. That asymmetry is the v2
   143	shape: under a transport with no reader surface the two directions are two
   144	streams, and pretending otherwise is what produced the hidden inbox.
   145	
   146	**Client state, explicitly:**
   147	
   148	```clojure
   149	{:writer      <handle>          ; request path
   150	 :reader      <handle>          ; response medium
   151	 :cursor      <cursor>          ; the successor next returned; never arithmetic
   152	 :me          <attachment-id>   ; nil on a private medium
   153	 :decode      <fn>              ; envelope -> response, or nil for bare values
   154	 :outstanding {id {:op … :args …}}
   155	 :unsent      nil-or-{:id … :op … :args …}
   156	 :completed   [ … ]}
   157	```
   158	
   159	- **`request!`** appends and returns the next state. On `:dao.stream/full`
   160	  nothing was appended: the request and **its already-allocated id** are
   161	  retained in `:unsent`, and the next `request!` retries that one rather than
   162	  minting a new id. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are terminal
   163	  for that request.
   164	- **`poll!`** drains the response medium up to a budget through its own cursor,
   165	  classifies each element, matches responses to `:outstanding`, and returns the
   166	  next state plus completions. `:dao.stream/blocked` returns to the caller; that
   167	  is the yield, and the caller must then return to its host loop.
   168	- **One driver owns client state and threads it serially.** Two callers holding
   169	  the same immutable state mint the same next id and produce divergent states.
   170	  Several requests may be outstanding at once — ids are matched at drain time,
   171	  which removes the whole bug class v1's `advance-cursor!` monotonic-max surgery
   172	  exists to patch — but only under one poller.
   173	- **Timeouts are the driver's, not the layer's.** A step-driven layer has no
   174	  clock; deadlines are the caller's, and `now` is passed in if the layer needs
   175	  it at all.
   176	- **Loss is conservative.** On a `:dao.stream/gap` the recovery cursor says
   177	  nothing about which responses were skipped, so **every outstanding request is
   178	  reported lost**, not a computed subset. The same applies on any terminal
   179	  lifecycle event for this attachment — `:ws/closed`, `:ws/not-found`,
   180	  `:ws/transport-error` — which must be reported as loss rather than left to
   181	  time out.
   182	- **Reattachment is a rebind.** `rebind` swaps a dead writer for a fresh
   183	  `attach!` result and takes the new `:me`, keeping the cursor. Without it the
   184	  client filters on a stale attachment id and drops everything the new boundary
   185	  deposits.
   186	
   187	**The response medium carries envelopes, not responses.** On a real socket every
   188	element is `{:ws/attachment … :ws/event … :ws/value …}`, mixed across
   189	attachments and interleaved with `:ws/opened`, `:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`,
   190	`:ws/not-found` and `:ws/transport-error`. `poll!` must filter to `:me`, advance
   191	past other attachments' events without treating them as responses, unwrap only
   192	`:ws/payload`, and convert lifecycle events into client transitions.
   193	`dao.stream.rpc.ws` supplies that `:decode` and consumes the **whole**
   194	`attach!` result including `:dao.stream/attachment`. R1 tested over bare ring
   195	buffers passes and then breaks at R3 unless this seam is built in R1.
   196	
   197	**Server state, explicitly:**
   198	
   199	```clojure
   200	{:request-cursor      <cursor>
   201	 :pending-response    nil-or-<encoded>
   202	 :pending-request-id  nil-or-<id>}
   203	```
   204	
   205	`serve-step` retries `:pending-response` **before** reading another request. A
   206	response `append!` that returns `full` must not advance the request cursor — and
   207	must not re-run the handler either, because Yin evaluation is stateful and
   208	re-execution is wrong. v1 sidesteps this by ignoring the write result
   209	(`rpc/server.cljc:65`); that cannot be copied into an API claiming totality.
   210	Each request advances the cursor once and executes its handler once.
   211	
   212	**The envelope is fixed in R1**: request and response value shapes, correlation
   213	id representation, and handler-error encoding, under `:dao.stream.rpc/…`
   214	keys. v1's `dao.stream.apply` maps (`apply.cljc:42-63`) and its `{:error msg}`
   215	wrapping (`rpc/server.cljc:24-38`) are the obvious model, copied under v2 keys
   216	rather than required from v1.
   217	
   218	**Not in the v2 layer:** `retry` and `dedup`, which exist for the lossy UDP
   219	transport that v2 does not have. They stay on v1 with `dao.stream.rpc.udp`.
   220	
   221	## The REPL driver
   222	
   223	The first draft asserted that the REPL "already owns a polling loop." It does
   224	not, and never did: `rpc-client/wait-for-response` owned it
   225	(`rpc/client.cljc:77-111`) and the REPL consumed its Promise or Future
     1	# DaoStream WebSocket Transport
     2	
     3	Status: design target, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`. That document is the
     4	contract; this one specifies the WebSocket transport's implementation of it.
     5	Where the two disagree, the contract wins.
     6	
     7	## What the Descriptor Names
     8	
     9	A WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.
    10	The stream exists on the serving host — created there, retained there,
    11	closed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not any client is
    12	currently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one attachment:
    13	connecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and neither
    14	event creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes `attach!`
    15	honest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because
    16	the stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire.
    17	
    18	This framing generalizes: **serving is what makes any stream remotely
    19	attachable.** A ring buffer is host-local only because nothing speaks for
    20	it. A host that composes a WebSocket endpoint in front of one is serving
    21	that ring buffer; its descriptor now carries an address, and remote
    22	interpreters can attach. The transport owns no stream — it serves streams
    23	the host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors
    24	resolve against "the streams a network endpoint serves because serving them
    25	is what it is."
    26	
    27	A server that wants per-conversation state does not bend attachment into
    28	creation: it `create!`s a fresh stream for the conversation and offers that
    29	stream's descriptor.
    30	
    31	## Surfaces
    32	
    33	A WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable**, plus `descriptor` as every
    34	handle. It has no reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make
    35	the reader surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence
    36	that cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.
    37	
    38	Both a client handle and a server-side accepted-connection handle answer
    39	`descriptor` with the **served stream's** descriptor — the logical stream the
    40	connection joined. A descriptor names a stream, never a connection, so there
    41	is nothing else for it to name; two connections to the same served stream
    42	answer identically, and `:dao.stream/attachment` is what tells them apart.
    43	
    44	The writer surface is on the connection's **ordered outbound path** — the
    45	sequence of values accepted for sending on this attachment — never on the
    46	served logical stream. `:dao.stream/ok` places the value in the outbound path;
    47	what becomes of it at the far end is reported there, not here (contract,
    48	Writing).
    49	
    50	Outcomes this transport produces, and why it excludes the rest:
    51	
    52	| Operation    | Produces                                       | Excluded, and why                                                                             |
    53	|--------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    54	| `create!`    | —                                              | Absent: the transport owns no stream. Dynamic dispatch for this type returns `not-found`.     |
    55	| `attach!`    | `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, `transport-error`  | `transport-error` covers only failures decidable here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that fails, local resources exhausted. `not-found`, and the reachability failures that need a remote answer, are displaced to the deposit medium. |
    56	| `descriptor` | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |
    57	| `append!`    | `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error` | — (the contract's whole set)                                                      |
    58	| `close!`     | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |
    59	| `cursor`, `next` | —                                          | No reader surface, per above.                                                                 |
    60	
    61	## The Duplex Model
    62	
    63	A WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the
    64	design keeps them separate:
    65	
    66	- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and
    67	  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.
    68	- **Inbound**: each arriving message is a host event. The host's adapter
    69	  deposits it — payloads and connection lifecycle events alike, judging
    70	  nothing — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this
    71	  boundary. The deposit medium is an ordinary DaoStream stream the host
    72	  composed; this transport defines no separate event mechanism and no bus of
    73	  its own. (In the v2 migration slice that stream is a ring buffer, under a
    74	  time-boxed exception recorded in ADR 0003; the adapter cannot tell what it
    75	  deposits into, so the later swap is composition-only.) From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,
    76	  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that
    77	  stream.
    78	
    79	The deposit destination is wired once, when the host composes the boundary —
    80	it is not a parameter of `attach!`, and no attaching caller chooses it. The
    81	composition that wires the boundary also hands the medium to whichever
    82	interpreter reads it, which may or may not be the code that called `attach!`. Every interpreter takes its own perspective on the same deposited
    83	truth: one indexes the traffic, another copies it to a file, a third ignores
    84	it. How arrivals are interpreted is the interpreter's business, not the
    85	transport's configuration.
    86	
    87	**Attachment identity.** This transport distinguishes its attachments, so its
    88	`attach!` success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` as the contract
    89	requires: a descriptor names the served stream and cannot tell two attachments
    90	to it apart, and a handle is a host-local object that cannot be compared
    91	against data that crossed a codec.
    92	
    93	Every event deposited for that attachment carries the same value under
    94	`:ws/attachment`. The key differs because the scopes differ: result keywords
    95	are the contract's and live under `:dao.stream/…`, while a deposited event is
    96	a stream element, where transport-owned keys live under the transport's
    97	namespace. Correlation is by the value carried, never by the key's name.
    98	
    99	Without that identity a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence with no
   100	demultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and unattributable.
   101	
   102	**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are
   103	distinguishable and both are attributable:
   104	
   105	```clojure
   106	{:ws/attachment <id>
   107	 :ws/event      :ws/payload  ; or :ws/opened :ws/closed :ws/ended
   108	                             ;    :ws/error :ws/not-found
   109	                             ;    :ws/transport-error
   110	 :ws/value      <decoded>}   ; payload events only
   111	```
   112	
   113	The event kinds divide into three groups. **Resolution** — how this attachment
   114	turned out: `:ws/opened` when the endpoint accepted the stream, `:ws/not-found`
   115	when it disclaimed it, `:ws/transport-error` when the endpoint could not be
   116	reached at all. Exactly one of these is deposited per attachment, and it is
   117	what a retry policy reads. **Lifecycle** — `:ws/closed` when the connection
   118	went away and `:ws/ended` when the served stream itself ended (see Ending a
   119	served stream). **Traffic** — `:ws/payload`, carrying `:ws/value`, and
   120	`:ws/error` for a socket-level error the connection survived. `:ws/error` is
   121	therefore never a resolution: it says something went wrong on a connection that
   122	had already resolved.
   123	
   124	**The adapter registers additively** — `addEventListener` on a browser socket,
   125	`.on` on a Node emitter — never by assigning the `on*` properties. Assignment
   126	is a single slot: whoever writes it last wins, so anyone reaching past the
   127	handle to the underlying socket and installing their own handler would silently
   128	unhook the deposit path for every interpreter reading the medium. Additive
   129	registration makes that impossible — a second handler is a second handler, not
   130	a replacement — and the property then holds whether or not anyone respects the
   131	handle's boundary.
   132	
   133	Wrapping is not judging — the adapter never looks inside `:ws/value`. Nothing
   134	of this crosses the wire: the receiving side already knows which socket
   135	delivered a message, so correlation is added locally, on both ends
   136	independently, and the wire carries bare payloads.
   137	
   138	**Granularity is the composition's choice.** One medium per boundary
   139	multiplexes every attachment onto one sequence and one retention window, so a
   140	flood from one attachment evicts another's events. One medium per attachment
   141	isolates them, but the boundary must then announce new attachments on a
   142	boundary-level lifecycle stream, since nothing discovers a stream by itself.
   143	Either shape carries the identity; only the sharing changes.
   144	
   145	```clojure
   146	(def result (ws/attach! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws
   147	                         :ws/host "example.org"
   148	                         :ws/port 9090
   149	                         :ws/path "/streams/telemetry"}))
   150	
   151	(def sock (:dao.stream/handle result))
   152	(def me   (:dao.stream/attachment result))   ; keep it — nothing else identifies
   153	                                             ; this attachment on the medium
   154	
   155	;; write: (append! sock msg)
   156	;; read:  interpreters observe the medium the host boundary deposits into,
   157	;;        keeping events whose :ws/attachment is `me`
   158	```
   159	
   160	Nothing accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket inbox would
   161	be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed, sized by
   162	nobody. Retention lives in the medium the boundary deposits into, composed
   163	and sized by the host.
   164	
   165	There are no callback hooks — no `on-open!` / `on-message!` / `on-close!`
   166	surface of the kind the host's own socket API offers. Every socket event
   167	becomes data on the deposit stream — payload and lifecycle (opened, closed by peer, error)
   168	alike, distinguishable by shape. A boundary deposits everything; whether
   169	anyone reads it is not the boundary's concern.
   170	
   171	### Serving
   172	
   173	Serving is the mirror of attaching, and the deposit model is the same on both
   174	ends: each accepted connection's inbound messages are deposited — enveloped
   175	with that connection's attachment identity, exactly as on the client — onto a
   176	stream the host composition wired.
   177	
   178	**That stream is not the served stream.** A client's `append!` is a request to
   179	the server, never a broadcast: what a client writes lands in a medium only the
   180	serving host's own interpreters read. Nothing a client sends reaches the other
   181	attachments by default.
   182	
   183	The alternative — depositing a client's messages straight into the served
   184	stream — would make every client a writer to the stream every other client is
   185	reading, by nothing more than holding a handle. A composition that wants that
   186	writes it explicitly: an interpreter reads the request medium, decides what
   187	qualifies, and appends to the served stream. Then the admission rule is a
   188	piece of code someone wrote, in one place, rather than a consequence of the
   189	transport's shape.
   190	
     1	# DaoStream Implementation Plan — the WebSocket Slice
     2	
     3	Status: migration plan, derived from and subordinate to `dao.stream.md`
     4	(the contract, review-approved) and `dao.stream.ws.md` (the WebSocket
     5	specification). Where this plan and the contract disagree, the contract
     6	wins. This document is transient: it is consumed as the phases complete,
     7	while the contract remains. Revised against the `gpt-5.6-sol` plan review
     8	of 2026-08-30 (`collab/review-dao-stream-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log`).
     9	
    10	## Strategy
    11	
    12	Two deliberate narrowings:
    13	
    14	1. **A `dao.stream` namespace, not an in-place rewrite.** The legacy
    15	   `dao.stream` has 52 `open!` call sites and every subsystem as a
    16	   consumer; rewriting it in place is an atomic big bang. The new
    17	   namespace is `dao.stream` — one new file,
    18	   `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` — building the contract greenfield while
    19	   legacy keeps the system running.
    20	   Consumers migrate one at a time, later, under their own plans. The
    21	   legacy implementation and its tests are evidence about behavior, not
    22	   constraints: no compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol.
    23	2. **The WebSocket slice, end to end, and nothing else.** One vertical
    24	   slice that forces the contract's riskiest promises early: the
    25	   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A
    26	   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable
    27	   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until
    28	   this slice stands.
    29	
    30	The slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream
    31	and the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is
    32	in scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy
    33	one happens to have.
    34	
    35	## Settled before Phase 1
    36	
    37	Three questions a phase would otherwise be forced to invent under delivery
    38	pressure. Each is decided here so no implementer decides it alone.
    39	
    40	**`attach!` is synchronous and never waits.** The Result Convention makes
    41	every operation return an outcome map; the contract's IO Model generalizes
    42	it — no operation waits. No transport in this slice returns a promise, a
    43	future, or a host-native async value from any of the seven operations, on
    44	any host. A ws `attach!` answers what is locally decidable — `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`,
    45	and `transport-error` for a handler that fails here and now — while how the
    46	connection resolved arrives as deposited
    47	lifecycle data and through the handle's own `append!` outcomes; see
    48	`dao.stream.ws.md`'s Operations section, corrected to match. A promise
    49	leaking out of a v2 operation is a defect, not a host accommodation.
    50	
    51	The rule covers `create!` equally, though this slice never exercises it:
    52	the ring buffer is in-memory and creates synchronously on every host, and
    53	the ws transport has no `create!` at all — it owns no stream, and starting
    54	an endpoint is a host composition step. A later transport whose creation
    55	needs host resources it cannot acquire synchronously (binding a port,
    56	opening a handle through an async-only API) answers the same way `attach!`
    57	does: the logical stream's identity exists at once, the handle reports
    58	acquisition state through its own operations, and the result of the
    59	acquisition arrives as data. Nothing waits.
    60	
    61	**Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated.** The contract's
    62	public surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy, and must
    63	not gain one — a predicate answer about a live handle is exactly what
    64	`closed?` was retired for. So the boundary constructor takes the deposit
    65	destination *and its declared retention nature as data*, derived from the
    66	same creation specification that produced the handle, and refuses a
    67	reject-mode declaration at assembly. This is configuration provenance, not
    68	runtime introspection. Runtime behavior is unchanged: every non-`ok`
    69	deposit result tears the connection down.
    70	
    71	**Logical-stream identity is minted in Phase 2.** `cursor-mismatch` in
    72	Phase 2 and stable descriptor identity in Phase 3 are the same identity.
    73	It is allocated when a logical stream is created, and both cursors and
    74	descriptors denote it. Their *representations* need not match and need not
    75	be equally serializable — cursor serialization across hosts stays TBD per
    76	the contract — but they must refer to the same thing.
    77	
    78	## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)
    79	
    80	The protocol and data surface, with no transport. The seven operations are
    81	two kinds, and the split is the point:
    82	
    83	- **Five handle operations**, as protocols a handle implements per its
    84	  declared surface: `descriptor` (universal identity), `cursor` and `next`
    85	  (reader), `append!` (writer), `close!` (closable).
    86	- **Two transport entry functions**, `create!` and `attach!`, which take no
    87	  handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions.
    88	  Dynamic selection is a host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to a
    89	  `{create attach}` pair — ordinary data, ordinary lookup, performed by the
    90	  host. DaoStream ships no dispatch machinery: no multimethod, no registry,
    91	  no load-time side effect. Host dispatch is documentation plus an example
    92	  map in tests.
    93	
    94	Putting `create!` or `attach!` on a protocol is how the retired registry
    95	grows back; the file must not do it.
    96	
    97	Also in Phase 1:
    98	
    99	- The result convention: every operation returns an outcome map keyed by
   100	  `:dao.stream/outcome`, open maps, all qualified keywords under
   101	  `:dao.stream/…`, per-operation closed outcome sets exactly as the
   102	  contract's tables state, with no outcome held open for a mechanism that does
   103	  not exist.
   104	- Generic envelope validation: `:dao.stream/type` present and qualified;
   105	  everything else transport-owned.
   106	- `descriptor` on every handle regardless of surface — identity is
   107	  universal and outlives an attachment, so it must not be bundled into the
   108	  reader or writer protocol.
   109	
   110	Deliverable: a contract-conformance test suite, **declaration-driven by
   111	construction**. Its input is a manifest — ordinary data, not code the harness
   112	inspects — carrying the transport's entry functions and handle factories, its
   113	declared surfaces, its per-operation outcome subsets with a reason for every
   114	exclusion, and a fixture able to induce each outcome it does declare.
   115	
   116	The harness runs the blocks the manifest licenses: result shapes, envelope
   117	validation, and descriptor identity always, since `descriptor` belongs to no
   118	surface; reader laws (cursor provenance — mint / successor / gap-recovery only
   119	— non-destructive multi-reader reads, anchors) only for a declared reader;
   120	writer laws only for a writer; **close laws only for a declared closable**,
   121	which the earlier "always" wrongly assumed of every handle.
   122	
   123	Two assertions come from the manifest rather than from any one transport: no
   124	observed outcome falls outside the declared subset, and for each declared
   125	outcome the fixture actually produces it. That is what makes the contract's
   126	"declare which outcomes you produce, and why you exclude the rest" checkable
   127	rather than aspirational — an exclusion whose substitute never materializes
   128	fails, and an outcome that never appears proves nothing on its own.
   129	
   130	The suite is *authored* in Phase 1 and first *demonstrated* in Phase 2.
   131	Phase 1 has no transport, so it proves nothing on its own; that is expected
   132	and is not a reason to defer writing it.
   133	
   134	## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)
   135	
   136	The reference reader+writer+closable transport, scoped to the contract:
   137	
   138	- Logical-stream identity minted at `create!`, per *Settled before Phase 1*.
   139	- Evict-oldest retention with declared capacity; `append!` never returns
   140	  `full`; monotonic retention; `gap` with earliest-retained-else-tail
   141	  recovery cursor. Eviction applies whatever any reader is doing — a
   142	  retention policy that waits for readers is forbidden, not deferred (see
   143	  *Boundary of this plan*).
   144	No high-water figure in the creation specification: the ring buffer holds no
   145	reader positions and so can never act on one. The threshold at which a reader
   146	considers itself behind is that reader's own configuration, belonging to
   147	whatever interpreter measures it — which this slice does not build.
   148	- Cursor anchors `:dao.stream/oldest` and `:dao.stream/newest`;
   149	  `invalid-anchor`; cursors carry the logical-stream identity
   150	  (`cursor-mismatch` / `invalid-cursor` detectable); valid across handles
   151	  of the same logical stream.
   152	- One coherent state per operation (single atom; one deref per `next`),
   153	  satisfying the Concurrency section.
   154	
   155	Explicitly absent: waiters, drain, take, seq views, `closed?`, **live
   156	resize** — compatible with the contract but proving nothing the slice needs,
   157	since fixed declared capacity is sufficient for deposit admission — and the
   158	transport-owned **`lag`** operation, whose only consumer was flow control (see
   159	*Not in this plan*). Both follow the slice.
   160	
   161	The Phase 1 conformance suite runs here for the first time against a transport
   162	declaring all three surfaces. Laws that need a resolvable descriptor wait for
   163	Phase 3's directory; everything else runs now. Ring-buffer-specific tests cover
   164	retention, anchors, and eviction reporting.
   165	
   166	## Phase 3 — Descriptor round trip
   167	
   168	The portable identity story, still in-memory, and constructible without
   169	WebSockets: the contract explicitly permits a host composition to keep a
   170	directory, which is what makes a ring buffer's descriptor resolvable on its
   171	own host.
   172	
   173	- `descriptor` on a ring buffer handle yields a plain-data envelope that
   174	  survives the transit codec structurally unchanged.
   175	- A **host-kept local directory** — a map from stream identity to live
   176	  handle, owned by the test composition, not by DaoStream and not by any
   177	  transport. It is deliberately test-only: the ws serving side in Phase 4
   178	  resolves `:ws/path` against its own served-stream table and does not
   179	  reuse this. Say so in the code, so nobody later mistakes it for
   180	  infrastructure.
   181	- Round-trip test: descriptor → encode → decode → `attach!` against that
   182	  composition → handle on the same logical stream; a kept cursor resumes
   183	  through the new handle. This is where the kept-cursor promise is proven,
   184	  on a transport that has a reader surface.
   185	- `not-found` for descriptors nothing backs.
   186	
   187	**Decision gate — descriptor key set.** The contract leaves the descriptor
   188	envelope TBD and this phase produces the evidence for settling it. Phase 4
   189	must not start against provisional keys: review the round trip's key set,
   190	settle it in `dao.stream.md`, and settle `:ws/…` in `dao.stream.ws.md`
   191	against it. Provisional keys carried into a wire format under delivery
   192	pressure become permanent by accident.
   193	
   194	**Decision gate — wire contract.** The ws spec's Handshake section says the
   195	wire's exact form is not specified yet, and its Deferred list holds the
   196	pieces: how the served stream's identity is presented on connect, the exact
   197	form of the authoritative disclaimer, the application-range close code that
   198	distinguishes an ended stream from a dropped connection, the value codec, and
   199	what a receiver does with wire input that fails to decode. 4a builds both
   200	ends of that wire and must not start before they are settled — in
   201	`dao.stream.ws.md`, where they live — for the same reason as the descriptor
   202	gate: until then two independently written implementations cannot
   203	interoperate, no wire-level conformance test can be written, and provisional
   204	choices carried into a wire format under delivery pressure become permanent
   205	by accident. The gate settles what the Deferred list already scopes; it adds
   206	nothing to that list and invents no protocol here.
   207	
   208	## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition
   209	
   210	Per `dao.stream.ws.md`, both ends. Three deliverables in three layers —
   211	transport, infrastructure, composition — deliberately separate:
   212	
   213	### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)
   214	
   215	- **Client side**: `attach!` connects and returns without waiting, answering
   216	  only what is decidable here and now — `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, or
   217	  `transport-error` for a handler that fails locally; see *Settled before
