    45	## The VM
    46	
    47	`yin.vm` consumes streams through v1 protocols: `engine/run-on-stream` polls the
    48	VM's `:in-stream` with v1 `ds/next` seeding `{:position 0}`
    49	(`yin/vm/stream_driver.cljc:25-26`), and `yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:281` appends
    50	with v1 `ds/append!`. A v2 handle implements none of that, so handing the v1 VM
    51	a v2 stream throws on the first evaluation.
    52	
    53	**The v2 REPL therefore runs on `yin.vm`**, specified in
    54	[`yin.vm.implementation-plan.md`](./yin.vm.implementation-plan.md) and a
    55	prerequisite of this plan. That is a second VM beside the first, ~3,142 lines,
    56	built on v2 streams throughout, requiring no v1 namespace.
    57	
    58	What follows from it:
    59	
    60	- **Datom-literal evaluation works**, because the v2 VM's in-stream is a v2 ring
    61	  buffer the REPL can append to.
    62	- **`(telemetry)` works**, in its local forms — a stream or stderr. `--telemetry`
    63	  and `--telemetry-stream` keep their local semantics; a `ws://` telemetry sink
    64	  is still out, as a second ws client that proves nothing `connect` does not.
    65	- **`(vm :type)` accepts only `:ast-walker`** until `semantic`, `register` and
    66	  `stack` are ported. The v2 REPL defaults to it. This is the one user-visible
    67	  reduction against v1, and it is a scope decision in the VM plan rather than a
    68	  limitation of the transport.
    69	
    70	An earlier draft of this plan took the opposite route — constructing the v1 VM
    71	with `:in-stream nil`, which `engine.cljc:310` guards on, and dropping
    72	datom-eval and telemetry from the slice. That works and is recorded here because
    73	it remains the fallback if `yin.vm` slips: the REPL can ship transport-first
    74	with a v1 VM and no VM-owned streams, then gain both features when the v2 VM
    75	lands. It is not the plan.
